//
// @FR-F-Spec — a spec the value's renderer cannot execute is REFUSED, not run.
//
// Two shapes, both of which reached the back end before anyone could say the spec was wrong.
//
// **A width that is not a number.**  The width slot parses a full expression, so a variable can
// supply it.  It used to accept whatever that expression produced.  `{n:0>5}` — how a reader
// coming from Rust spells zero-pad-right — parses as the comparison `0 > 5`, and a BOOLEAN went
// to the width: `--interpret` rendered the number with no padding at all and said nothing,
// while `--native` handed the bool to `ops::format_long_with_tag` and the program failed with
// rustc's own `E0308 expected i64, found bool`, naming loft's internals rather than the spec.
//
// It is the residual of a defect already fixed one position over.  `string_states` was made to
// consume the FLAGS in any order precisely because an out-of-order flag was "simply left in the
// stream for the WIDTH expression to find" — and a `0` fill is left there for the same reason,
// because the pad-character branch can only claim a token and a digit lexes as an Integer.
//
// **A radix the renderer has no arm for.**  `ops::format_long` implements the four radixes the
// rule lists and ends in `panic!("Unknown radix")`.  The spec reader answers two more, `e`
// (scientific) and `j` (JSON), neither of which means anything for an integer — so
// `println("{n:e}")`, an ordinary source program with no unsafe corner in it, aborted the
// interpreter on a Rust panic.
//
// Both are refusals rather than silent behaviour on purpose: L9's rule beside these arms
// already says that *a specifier that can never have any effect on the value type is always a
// bug*, and the rule was right — it had simply only ever been asked about `text` and `boolean`.
//
// @falsified-at: d9aa10ac — interpret exit 1 -> 0, native exit 1 -> 0.  The EXIT channel is
// what scores this file and the `refusals` column reads 0 on both trees, which is not a miss:
// the count comes from a plain run, and a file with no `main` runs nothing there.  Under
// `--tests` the control fails because three of these four errors do not exist on it — `{n:0>5}`
// compiles silently and `{n:e}` reaches its panic only at run time, which no `@EXPECT_ERROR`
// can see — so the file is red there and green here.
//
// The rule is asked of EVERY renderer, not only the integer one.  It was the integer one for as
// long as the refusals were spelled as a list of two types, and a list is the shape that cannot
// say what it left out: a radix meant nothing on a float, a single, a character or a vector and
// each of them dropped it in silence, while a PRECISION meant nothing anywhere but a float and
// a single and was worse than dropped — the dotted `{n:8.2}` left an `f64` in a slot the opcode
// reads as an i64 width, which is ~4.6e18 pad characters and an OOM-killed process, and the bare
// `{n:.4}` quietly rendered a four-wide field instead.  So the question is now asked as "which
// radixes does THIS renderer have an arm for", which every type answers.
//   HOLDS: assertions, leak, panic, free-refusal are equal on both trees. Falsify prints only
//   the channels that MOVE and requires HERE to be clean, so each of those is clean on both.
//   The native side was not scored in this sweep.

// @EXPECT_ERROR: a format width must be a number
fn test_a_comparison_is_not_a_width() {
  n = 7;
  println("{n:0>5}");
}

// @EXPECT_ERROR: a format width must be a number
fn test_a_comparison_is_not_a_width_with_a_non_zero_fill() {
  n = 7;
  println("{n:5>3}");
}

// @EXPECT_ERROR: is not an integer format
fn test_scientific_is_not_an_integer_radix() {
  n = 255;
  println("{n:e}");
}

// @EXPECT_ERROR: is not an integer format
fn test_json_is_not_an_integer_radix() {
  n = 255;
  println("{n:j}");
}

// @falsified-at (the eight cells below): checked ONE AT A TIME, in its own file, in BOTH
// directions — loft#1261 credits every declaration after the first firing one, so a
// whole-file run reports n/n on a build that produces one of them.  All eight fire on this
// tree and all eight were accepted in SILENCE on 5792ff53:
//
//   {f:x} -> 2.5     {f:b} single -> 2.5    {c:x} -> A       {v:o} -> [1,2]
//   {n:.4} -> 1      {t:.2} -> abcdef
//   {n:8.2} and {v:8.2} -> neither: the process was OOM-killed asking for 4 GiB in one
//   allocation, which is why the two dotted cells are the reason this walk happened.

// ── A radix reaches a renderer with an arm for it ────────────────────────────────────────
// An integer has the four bases plus upper-case hex; a vector, struct or enum has the JSON
// switch; every other type renders exactly one way, so only the decimal default reaches it.

// @EXPECT_ERROR: `x` has no effect on float
fn test_a_base_has_no_meaning_for_a_float() {
  ratio = 2.5;
  println("{ratio:x}");
}

// @EXPECT_ERROR: `b` has no effect on single
fn test_a_base_has_no_meaning_for_a_single() {
  ratio = 2.5f;
  println("{ratio:b}");
}

// @EXPECT_ERROR: `x` has no effect on character
fn test_a_base_has_no_meaning_for_a_character() {
  initial = 'A';
  println("{initial:x}");
}

// @EXPECT_ERROR: `o` has no effect on vector<integer>
fn test_a_base_has_no_meaning_for_a_vector() {
  counts = [1, 2];
  println("{counts:o}");
}

// ── A precision reaches only the two types that have fractional digits ───────────────────
// Both spellings are refused: the dotted `W.P`, and the bare `.P` that gives no width.

// @EXPECT_ERROR: a precision has no effect on integer
fn test_a_dotted_spec_is_not_an_integer_width() {
  total = 1;
  println("{total:8.2}");
}

// @EXPECT_ERROR: a precision has no effect on integer
fn test_a_bare_precision_is_not_an_integer_width() {
  total = 1;
  println("{total:.4}");
}

// @EXPECT_ERROR: a precision has no effect on text
fn test_a_precision_does_not_truncate_text() {
  label = "abcdef";
  println("{label:.2}");
}

// @EXPECT_ERROR: a precision has no effect on vector<integer>
fn test_a_precision_has_no_meaning_for_a_vector() {
  counts = [1, 2];
  println("{counts:8.2}");
}
